<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vatican City</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Vatican City State, Città del Vaticano, Status Civitatis Vaticanae</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vatican City is a sovereign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a>, an enclave within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, ruled by the pope and governed by the Holy See as the administrative center of the Catholic Church. Catholic theology holds that Saint Peter was crucified on Vatican Hill in the 1st century. The Holy See ruled the Papal States, formed in 756, until the capture of Rome by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1870, after which the pope declared himself a prisoner in the Vatican; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lateran-treaty">Lateran Treaty</a> of 1929 created the modern state. With 0.49 square kilometres and about 882 people it is the smallest country by area and population, and it holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vatican-city.jpg" alt="Vatican City" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0_Basilique_Saint-Pierre_-_Rome_(1).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Lateran Treaty created the <b>Vatican City</b> State, which still exists.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Their loss in 1870 freed the papacy from the burdens of territorial government and, after the Lateran Treaty of 1929, left it with the small sovereign state of <b>Vatican City</b>, which allows the Holy See to act in international affairs as a sovereign entity today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Monaco is 2.08 square kilometres of hillside and reclaimed shore on the French Riviera, the second-smallest sovereign state after <b>Vatican City</b> and the most densely populated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-monaco">Principality of Monaco · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The pope rules <b>Vatican City</b> as an elected absolute monarch.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vatican City</b> was created by the Lateran Treaty of 1929; Monaco has been ruled by the Grimaldi family since 1297, with brief interruptions; and Singapore, which left the Federation of Malaysia in 1965, has been called by The Economist the “world’s only fully functioning city-state”.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The popes, monarchs of the Papal States until 1870 and of <b>Vatican City</b> since 1929, were elected by the cardinals from 1059, by a two-thirds majority from 1179 and, under a rule first imposed in 1274, behind locked doors.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
